How about adding a new item (or rather one for each class of objects) to 
the game, which for the sake of argument we could call a 'spircle'.

An unidentified spircle would have the appearance of some other 
unidentified object in your inventory. When a spircle is randomly 
generated, the RNG will make a certain number of attempts to generate 
another random object and if the object is not fully identified give the 
spircle that object's description. If it cannot find an unidentified object 
to emulate the new spircle will look like something previously identified 
instead.

A hostile djinn may pretend to give you a wish and generate a spircle, or a 
wish when your luck is negative may generate a spircle. In each case it 
should look like the wished-for object (even if the object is identified).

Spircles take on some of the aspects of the objects they mimic: armour and 
amulet spircles may be worn, scroll spircles may be read, potion and food 
spircles may be consumed. The effect of reading/quaffing/dipping into a 
spircle would be a random scroll/potion effect. Zapping a wand spircle 
should have a very high probability of also destroying it. Wearing a 
spircle would initially give you the armour class you expect from the 
emulated object, but the armour class would gradually wear off (similar to 
protection wearing off) until it reaches +0 (or -5 if cursed). Enchant 
armour on a spircle would increase the armour class but also wear off 
gradually.

Eating a food spircle has no nutritional or intrinsic granting value but 
may have some of the less good food effects. Spircles cannot be tinned. 
Applying a spircle figurine gives you a random, non-tame creature. Spircle 
spellbooks would 'make no sense' if successfully read and no spell, but can 
still have the bad effects of reading.

Identifying a spircle identifies it as a spircle in the appropriate object 
class, names it 'looking like '+whatever its previous description, and 
autocurses it.
Identifying an emulated object doesn't change a spircle's description. 

Polymorphing a spircle it remains a spircle although it may change class 
and description.

Spircles don't stack, which may make some of them easier to identify. If 
unidentified they should fool shopkeepers into valuing them by their 
appearance most of the time, and be identified by and anger a shopkeeper 
some of the time. Of course if selling a spircle the shopkeeper prices it 
by its appearance.

Randomly generate spircles should be very rare, so that most of the time it 
isn't worth taking extra steps to avoid their effects unless you are really 
paranoid.


